tags.description:
To create a post you first need to get the social accounts you want to post to, upload any media for the post (if applicable), and then create the post. 

You only need to get the social accounts once as the ids will not change, however you will need to upload media each time you create a post.

The below example shows how to create a media post from start to finish.

**Example flow using JavaScript and the Fetch API:**

**Get Social Accounts**

   ```js
   // Step 1: Fetch the social accounts you want to post to. In this case we are getting all facebook and instagram accounts.
   const socialAccountResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/social-accounts?platform=facebook&platform=instagram', {
     method: 'GET',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     }
   });

   const { data } = await socialAccountResponse.json();

   //Grab the ids from the social account response
   const accountIds =  data.map((account) => account.id);
   ```

**Create a signed upload url for the media we are posting**

   ```js
   // Step 2: Request an upload URL 
   const file = fs.readFileSync('path-to-your-file');
   const mediaResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/media/create-upload-url', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       name: 'photo.jpg',
       mime_type: 'image/jpeg',
       size_bytes: file.length
     })
   });

   //Save the media_id for the post, and the upload_url for uploading the file
   const { media_id, upload_url } = await mediaResponse.json();
   ```

**Upload the file to the upload url**

 ```js
   // Step 3: Upload the file to the upload url 
   const fileUploadResponse = await fetch(upload_url, {
    method: 'PUT',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'image/jpeg',
    },
    body: file,
  });
   ```

**Create the post**

 ```js
   // Step 4: Create the post using the social account ids and the media_id

   // We are scheduling the post one hour from now
   const scheduledAt = new Date();
   scheduledAt.setHours(scheduledAt.getHours() + 1);

   const postResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/posts', {
       method: 'POST',
       headers: {
         'Content-Type': 'application/json',
         'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
       },
       body: JSON.stringify({
         caption: 'Hello, world!',
         scheduled_at: scheduledAt,
         media: [media_id],
         social_accounts: accountIds,
       }),
     });

     const postData = await postResponse.json();
   ```

**Thats It! You have successfully scheduled a post to go out in one hour.**

---

**Current API limits**

- API keys currently have a general limit of **10 requests per second per key**.
- There is no bulk or batch endpoint for creating multiple posts or requesting multiple upload URLs in one API call.
- If you need higher throughput, throttle your client so the aggregate request rate for that key stays under the limit and retry `429` responses with backoff.

---

**Using a custom cover image for Instagram Reels**

You can set a custom cover image for Instagram video posts by uploading an image via the media endpoint and passing its `media_id` as `cover_image` in the Instagram platform configuration.

 ```js
   // Upload a cover image using the same media upload flow (Steps 2 & 3 above)
   const coverResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/media/create-upload-url', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       name: 'cover.jpg',
       mime_type: 'image/jpeg',
       size_bytes: coverFile.length
     })
   });

   const { media_id: cover_media_id, upload_url: cover_upload_url } = await coverResponse.json();

   // Upload the cover image file
   await fetch(cover_upload_url, {
     method: 'PUT',
     headers: { 'Content-Type': 'image/jpeg' },
     body: coverFile,
   });

   // Create the post with the cover image
   const postResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/posts', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       caption: 'Check out this reel!',
       media: [video_media_id],
       social_accounts: accountIds,
       platform_configurations: {
         instagram: {
           cover_image: cover_media_id
         }
       }
     }),
   });
   ```

---

**Posting as a Story (Instagram or Facebook)**

To publish as a Story instead of a normal feed post, set `placement: "story"` in the platform configuration. Stories require **exactly one** image or video. Instagram Stories don't support captions; Facebook Stories don't support captions or carousels.

 ```js
   // Upload one image or video first (Steps 2 & 3 from the example above)

   const postResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/posts', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       scheduled_at: scheduledAt,
       media: [media_id], // Exactly one
       social_accounts: accountIds,
       platform_configurations: {
         instagram: { placement: 'story' },
         facebook: { placement: 'story' }
       }
     }),
   });
   ```

---

**YouTube tags**

Pass `tags` (an array of strings) in the YouTube platform configuration to set the video's tags per post, e.g. `youtube: { tags: ['cooking', 'easy recipes'] }`. YouTube caps the whole set at 500 characters.

**Disclosing AI-generated content (YouTube)**

If your video contains realistic altered or synthetic (AI-generated) content, set `contains_synthetic_media: true` in the YouTube platform configuration. YouTube may display an "Altered or synthetic content" label to viewers. This maps to the YouTube Data API `status.containsSyntheticMedia` field.

**Disclosing paid promotion (YouTube)**

If your video includes paid product placement, a sponsorship or an endorsement, set `has_paid_product_placement: true` in the YouTube platform configuration. YouTube shows viewers an "Includes paid promotion" label. This maps to the YouTube Data API `paidProductPlacementDetails.hasPaidProductPlacement` field.

 ```js
   const postResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/posts', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       caption: 'Check out this AI-generated video!',
       media: [video_media_id],
       social_accounts: accountIds,
       platform_configurations: {
         youtube: { contains_synthetic_media: true }
       }
     }),
   });
   ```

---

**Setting a custom thumbnail (YouTube)**

For regular (long-form) videos, you can set a custom thumbnail by uploading an image via the media endpoint and passing its `media_id` as `thumbnail` in the YouTube platform configuration. This only applies to standard horizontal videos — YouTube ignores custom thumbnails on Shorts (vertical/short videos). The connected YouTube channel must be verified to use custom thumbnails, and the image should be JPEG/PNG, 1280x720, under 2MB. If the thumbnail can't be applied, the video is still published.

 ```js
   // Upload a thumbnail image using the same media upload flow (Steps 2 & 3 above)
   const thumbResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/media/create-upload-url', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       name: 'thumbnail.jpg',
       mime_type: 'image/jpeg',
       size_bytes: thumbnailFile.length
     })
   });

   const { media_id: thumbnail_media_id, upload_url: thumbnail_upload_url } = await thumbResponse.json();

   // Upload the thumbnail image file
   await fetch(thumbnail_upload_url, {
     method: 'PUT',
     headers: { 'Content-Type': 'image/jpeg' },
     body: thumbnailFile,
   });

   // Create the post with the custom thumbnail
   const postResponse = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/posts', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       caption: 'My latest long-form video',
       media: [video_media_id],
       social_accounts: accountIds,
       platform_configurations: {
         youtube: {
           thumbnail: thumbnail_media_id
         }
       }
     }),
   });
   ```

post.description:
To upload media to attach to your post, make a `POST` request to the `/media/create-upload-url` endpoint. 

You'll receive the ID of your media item (which can be used anywhere that `media_id` is referenced) and will include an `upload_url` which is a signed URL of the storage location for uploading your file to. 

This URL is unique and publicly signed for a short time, so make sure to upload your files in a timely manner.

**Example flow using JavaScript and the Fetch API:**

**Request an upload URL**

   ```js
   // Step 1: Request an upload URL from your API
   const response = await fetch('https://api.post-bridge.com/v1/media/create-upload-url', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json'
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({
       name: 'photo.jpg',
       mime_type: 'image/jpeg',
       size_bytes: 123456
     })
   });

   const { media_id, upload_url } = await response.json();
   ```

**Upload your file to the signed URL**

   ```js
   // Step 2: Upload your file to the signed URL
   const file = /* your File or Blob object, e.g., from an <input type="file"> */;
   await fetch(upload_url, {
     method: 'PUT',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'image/jpeg'
     },
     body: file
   });
   ```

**Use the `media_id` in your post or wherever a media reference is required.**

**Rate limit note**

- API keys currently have a general limit of **10 requests per second per key**.
- There is no bulk endpoint for requesting multiple upload URLs at once.
- If you are uploading many files, keep your aggregate request rate under the limit and retry `429` responses with backoff.
